<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <!--
    Presenters, and the view interfaces they drive. The Presenter half of MVP (D54, D55).

    **The absence of a WinForms reference IS the design.** D54 records why MVP was chosen over
    MVVM in the first place, and it was not familiarity: MVP's boundary can be made a compiler
    error rather than a convention someone has to remember. A presenter here that reaches for a
    Button, a Control or a Form does not compile, and no comment, review or convention is doing
    that work.

    That is also why this is plain net10.0 with no reference to Render or Viewer3D. It depends on
    Core alone - the model - so a presenter cannot quietly acquire a dependency on how anything is
    drawn, and every test over this project runs on the Linux measurement boxes.

    A presenter here is a plain class: it holds the model, subscribes to a view interface's events,
    and pushes results back through that interface's methods. The Form implements the interface and
    does nothing else - "if a Form method needs an `if` statement about business state, that's the
    tell it's doing the Presenter's job" (D55).
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
    <RootNamespace>Tf2DemoSalvage.Presentation</RootNamespace>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <InternalsVisibleTo Include="Tf2DemoSalvage.Presentation.Tests" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Tf2DemoSalvage.Core\Tf2DemoSalvage.Core.csproj" />

    <!-- **A presenter uses services, which is the direction that keeps this acyclic** (D54, D62).
         Audio references Core and Content and knows nothing about presenters, so this adds no
         cycle — and SoundSchedule already lived here, so the alternative was Audio reaching UP into
         the presenter layer to find it.

         Added 2026-08-25 with SoundPresenter, which came out of MainForm.PlaySounds (B188). -->
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Tf2DemoSalvage.Audio\Tf2DemoSalvage.Audio.csproj" />

    <!-- **A presenter uses the model**, which is the layer direction MVP asks for and the one that
         stays acyclic: Scene references Core, Content, Animation and Logging, and knows nothing
         about presenters.

         Added 2026-08-25 with FreeCameraController (D90). The camera needs MapOutline from Scene
         and OverheadPlacement from here, which is itself the answer to where it belongs. -->
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Tf2DemoSalvage.Scene\Tf2DemoSalvage.Scene.csproj" />

    <!-- **A presenter produces what a renderer draws**, so the draw-data types are downstream of
         it the same way Scene and Audio are. Render references Core, Content and Scene and knows
         nothing about presenters, so this adds no cycle.

         Added 2026-08-25 with FpsOverlay, out of MainForm.BuildHud (B188, D90). Render is
         `net10.0` — Direct3D arrives through Silk.NET at RUNTIME, not at compile time — so this
         does not pin the presenter layer to Windows and the port test in D90 still holds. -->
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Tf2DemoSalvage.Render\Tf2DemoSalvage.Render.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
